12_25_23_18_15	Debug: {'system_prompt': '\n        You are a Fight The Landlord (Dou Dizhu) player. \n        ### Introduction\n        Fight the Landlord (Dou Dizhu) is a poker climbing game primarily for three players.\n        ### Players, Cards and Deal\n        This game uses a 54-card pack including two jokers, red and black. The cards rank from high to low:\n        red joker, black joker, 2, A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3. Suits are irrelevant.\n\n        ### Play\n        The landlord plays first, and may play a single card or any legal combination.\n        Each subsequent player in anticlockwise order must either pass (play no card) or beat the previous play\n        by playing a higher combination of the same number of cards and same type.\n        There are just two exceptions to this: a rocket can beat any combination, and a bomb can beat any combination\n        except a higher bomb or rocket.\n\n        ### Combinations\n        1. Single card: ranking from three (low) up to red joker (high).\n        2. Pair: two cards of the same rank, from three (low) up to two (high).\n        3. Triplet: three cards of the same rank.\n        4. Triplet with an attached card: a triplet with any single card added.\n        5. Triplet with an attached pair: a triplet with a pair added.\n        6. Sequence: at least five cards of consecutive rank, from 3 up to ace. Twos and jokers cannot be used.\n        7. Sequence of pairs: at least three pairs of consecutive ranks, from 3 up to ace. Twos and jokers cannot be used.\n        8. Sequence of triplets: at least two triplets of consecutive ranks from three up to ace.\n        9. Sequence of triplets with attached cards: an extra card is added to each triplet.\n        10. Sequence of triplets with attached pairs: an extra pair is attached to each triplet.\n        11. Bomb: four cards of the same rank. Beats everything except a rocket and a higher bomb.\n        12. Rocket: a pair of jokers. The highest combination, beats everything.\n        13. Quadplex set: a quad with two single cards OR two pairs attached.\n        Use the EXACT card symbols provided in the Valid Moves list.\n        Prepare a valid JSON object like {"cards": ["♠3", "♥3"]} for your answer, along with your explanation and reason. If you pass, output: {"cards": []}\n        \n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output the action with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n    ', 'user_prompt': "You are playing as a peasant. Your aim is to be the play out all your cards before the landlord does, OR help your teammate (the other peasant) play out their cards before the landlord does. If the landlord plays out all cards first, you lose.\nYou are a Peasant. The Previous Player (Left) is the Landlord (ENEMY). The Next Player (Right) is your TEAMMATE (Peasant). You must cooperate with your teammate to beat the Landlord. The objective is to empty your hand before the landlord does, or help your teammate empty their hand before the landlord does.\n\nYour Hand: ♠3 ♠5 ♦5 ♥6 ♠7 ♥7 ♠8 ♦8 ♠9 ♣9 ♥10 ♣Q ♦K ♦A ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\nLast Play to Beat: ♥8 ♣8 ♠K ♥K ♣K\n--- Game History (Recent) ---\nTurn 1 (Prev Player): ♥8 ♣8 ♠K ♥K ♣K\n--- Player Info ---\nCards Held by Next Player (Right) [Peasant (Teammate)]: 17\nCards Held by Prev Player (Left) [Landlord (Opponent)]: 15\n--- Cards Remaining Unknown (Card Counting) ---\n(Format explanation: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nRedJoker: 1, BlackJoker: 1, 2: 1, A: 3, Q: 3, J: 4, 10: 3, 9: 2, 7: 2, 6: 3, 5: 2, 4: 4, 3: 3\nValid Moves:\ntriple_with_pair: ♠5 ♦5 ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\ntriple_with_pair: ♠7 ♥7 ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\ntriple_with_pair: ♠8 ♦8 ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\ntriple_with_pair: ♠9 ♣9 ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\npass: []\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation: The landlord just played a bomb: ♥8 ♣8 ♠K ♥K ♣K — this is a quad (four of a kind: 8s and Ks combined into an illegal combination? Wait — actually, this is not a legal bomb. A bomb must be four cards of the same rank. But here, the landlord played two 8s and three Ks — that’s five cards: two 8s and three Ks. That is not a valid bomb nor a valid combination.\n\nWait — let\'s reevaluate.\n\nThe play: ♥8 ♣8 ♠K ♥K ♣K — that’s two 8s and three kings. That is not a valid combination in Dou Dizhu.\n\nBut hold on — this might be a typo or misrepresentation. However, per standard rules, this cannot be a legal play unless it\'s interpreted differently.\n\nWait — perhaps it\'s a mistake. Alternatively, could it be that the landlord played a **full house**? But full house isn\'t a valid combo in standard Dou Dizhu.\n\nIn standard Fight the Landlord:\n\n- Full houses are **not allowed**.\n- Only valid combos are singles, pairs, triplets, triplet + single, triplet + pair, sequences, bombs, rockets, etc.\n\nSo playing two 8s and three Ks is **not a valid combination**.\n\nBut since the game system says "Last Play to Beat: ♥8 ♣8 ♠K ♥K ♣K", and we are expected to respond, we must assume this was accepted as a valid move — which suggests it may be a **bomb of kings**? But only three kings were played — not four.\n\nThree kings = triplet, not a bomb.\n\nTwo 8s = a pair.\n\nSo combining them isn’t a legal combo.\n\nThis suggests there may be an error in logging.\n\nAlternatively — is it possible the landlord played **a triplet of kings with a pair of 8s attached**? That would be a **"triplet with attached pair"**, which requires exactly **three of one rank and two of another** — yes! That fits: ♠K ♥K ♣K (triplet K) + ♥8 ♣8 (pair 8). So this is a **valid "triple_with_pair"** combination.\n\nAh — that makes sense. So the landlord played **triplet K with attached pair 8**: total five cards.\n\nNow, to beat it, I must play a **higher triplet_with_pair**.\n\nPer rules: a triplet_with_pair can only be beaten by a higher-ranked triplet_with_pair.\n\nRank comparison is based on the triplet part. So to beat triplet K, I need a **triplet A or triplet 2**.\n\nDo I have that?\n\nLooking at my hand:\n\n♠3 ♠5 ♦5 ♥6 ♠7 ♥7 ♠8 ♦8 ♠9 ♣9 ♥10 ♣Q ♦K ♦A ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\n\nI have:\n\n- ♦A → one Ace\n- ♠2, ♣2, ♦2 → three 2s\n- No other triplets except possibly building from 2s.\n\nI have three 2s: that’s a triplet of 2s.\n\nAnd I have many extra cards.\n\nTo form a **triplet_with_pair**, I need:\n\n- Three cards of one rank (triplet), and\n- Two cards of another rank (pair)\n\nI can use ♠2 ♣2 ♦2 as the triplet (rank 2 — highest possible), and attach any pair.\n\nAvailable pairs in my hand:\n\n- ♠5 ♦5 → pair of 5s ✅\n- ♠7 ♥7 → pair of 7s ✅\n- ♠8 ♦8 → pair of 8s ✅\n- ♠9 ♣9 → pair of 9s ✅\n\nAll of these are usable.\n\nSince the current play has a triplet of K, I can beat it with triplet of 2 (which is higher than K).\n\nAny of the above combinations would work.\n\nNow, which one should I choose?\n\nConsider strategy:\n\n- I want to get rid of low/middle cards early if possible, especially ones that are hard for me to play later.\n- My hand has no jokers, no second Ace, only one Q, one 10, etc.\n- I have three 2s — very powerful, but also risky to hold because they’re high and can only be played in limited combos.\n- Playing them now in a winning move is good.\n\nBut: should I save the 2s?\n\nOnly if I suspect the landlord or teammate might play a bomb or rocket later — but right now, the landlord just played a strong card (triplet K + pair 8), suggesting they may not have a bomb or rocket yet.\n\nAlso, I have **no bomb** (need four of a kind) — I don’t have four of anything.\n\nI do not have a rocket (jokers) — neither red nor black.\n\nSo I cannot beat a bomb or rocket anyway.\n\nTherefore, using my 2s now in a high combo is acceptable.\n\nNow, which attached pair to use?\n\nOptions:\n\n1. ♠2 ♣2 ♦2 + ♠5 ♦5 → uses both 5s\n2. ... + ♠7 ♥7\n3. ... + ♠8 ♦8\n4. ... + ♠9 ♣9\n\nWhich is best?\n\nLet’s consider card value:\n\n- 5s are low — better to get rid of them.\n- 7s, 8s, 9s are mid-range — also okay to discard.\n\nBut: if I play a higher attached pair, does it matter? No — the attached pair doesn\'t affect strength beyond being a valid pair. Only the triplet rank matters.\n\nSo all are equally effective in beating the play.\n\nBest choice: use a pair that is less useful later.\n\nI have duplicates in 5,7,8,9 — all are paired.\n\nBut note: I have only one of each of higher cards like Q, 10, K, A — so those are harder to pair.\n\nBut 5s are lowest — most likely to be wasted later.\n\nSo best to offload 5s.\n\nThus: use ♠5 ♦5 as the attached pair.\n\nCombo: ♠2 ♣2 ♦2 ♠5 ♦5 → triplet 2 with pair 5.\n\nThis is a valid move and beats the landlord’s triplet K + pair 8.\n\nWhy not save 2s? Because:\n\n- I cannot beat bombs or rockets anyway.\n- If I pass, my teammate (right) might have to play — and they have 17 cards, so likely not close to winning.\n- I have a rare chance to take control and disrupt the landlord.\n- This move reduces my hand from 16 to 11 cards — significant progress.\n- It removes low-value deadwood (5s) and uses powerful 2s effectively.\n\nAlternative: pass?\n\nBut I **can** beat it — and as a peasant, when you can block the landlord and help your teammate, you should — especially if it costs little.\n\nPassing would allow the landlord to continue their sequence unchecked.\n\nMoreover, if I don’t play now, I may never get another chance to use 2s constructively.\n\nThus, best to play.\n\nFinal choice: triplet 2 with pair 5 → ♠2 ♣2 ♦2 ♠5 ♦5\n\nCheck Valid Moves list:\n\nValid Moves include:\n- triple_with_pair: ♠5 ♦5 ♠2 ♣2 ♦2 → same cards, different order.\n\nYes — this is listed.\n\nOrder doesn\'t matter — as long as cards match.\n\nSo this is acceptable.\n\nAnswer must use exact symbols from the list.\n\nThe listed move is: ♠5 ♦5 ♠2 ♣2 ♦2 — same cards.\n\nSo I can output that.\n\nEvaluation: I can beat the landlord’s triplet K with pair 8 by playing a higher triplet_with_pair using triplet 2 (highest possible) and attaching a low pair (5s) to dispose of weak cards. This move pressures the landlord, helps my teammate by blocking progress, and efficiently uses high cards before they become deadweight.\n\nReason: Using triplet 2 ensures the play cannot be beaten by the landlord unless they have a bomb or rocket. Given that they played a strong non-bomb move, they likely don’t have a bomb (or would’ve used it). This gives us control. Attaching the 5s clears low, potentially useless cards. Passing would waste a key opportunity to disrupt the landlord.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠5", "♦5", "♠2", "♣2", "♦2"]}'}
